:ruby
  PageHeader = import("/components/PageHeader.haml")
  DocsPage = import("/components/DocsPage.haml")
  DocsSection = import("/components/DocsSection.haml")

%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Docs",
    title: "Data Imports",
    summary: "Built-in data plugins turn structured files and text files into Ruby modules with serializable default exports."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Built-In Data Formats" }
    :markdown
      `DataPlugin` is the base for Klenod's built-in data import plugins:

      - `JsonPlugin` handles `.json` with `JSON.parse`.
      - `YamlPlugin` handles `.yaml` and `.yml` with `YAML.safe_load`.
      - `TomlPlugin` handles `.toml` with `TomlRB.parse`.
      - `TextPlugin` handles `.txt` and `.text` as strings.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Import Values" }
    :markdown
      Data files transform into Ruby modules exporting `Default`. Development imports return the parsed value directly.

      ```ruby
      Metrics = import("./metrics.json")
      Navigation = import("./navigation.yaml")
      Release = import("./release.toml")
      Note = import("./note.txt")
      ```

      In runtime bundles, parsed data is serialized directly so production loading does not need the build plugin.

      Dates and times import as plain Ruby values in development and in bundles:

      - YAML and TOML dates become `Date`.
      - Datetimes with an offset become `Time` with that offset.
      - TOML local datetimes and local times become UTC `Time` values with the same clock time. A local time falls on 1970-01-01.
      - TOML `inf` and `nan` become `Float::INFINITY` and `Float::NAN`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "When To Use Data Imports" }
    :markdown
      Use data imports for content and configuration that should participate in the dependency graph, invalidate on file changes, and serialize into runtime bundles. Use Ruby modules when the value needs Ruby behavior, dynamic computation, or explicit methods.

